To facilitate accurate marking, your submitted solution must use this template.

To complete each part of the exercise, add your own code to the following files:
part1: nothing to do, this is example code (part1.c, chardev.c). It is provided as an example only and may need to be modified in order for the other parts to work properly.
part2: nothing to do, this solution is provided (KEY_SW.c)
part3: LEDR_HEX.c
part4: part4.c
additional part: partAdditional.c


Please test your solution using the provided runall.sh script:
part1: ./runall.sh 1 (to build and run the provided solution)
part2: ./runall.sh 2
part3: ./runall.sh 3
part4: ./runall.sh 4
additional part: ./runall.sh additional


ADDITIONAL PART (optional, 1 mark)
Take your part 3 driver or your part 4 program and add something new to it.
The partAdditional folder starts empty. This is where your additional part goes.

1. Put your .c file(s) in partAdditional. Any file name is fine.
   Start from a copy of your part 3 or part 4 code and change it.

2. Keep only your changed version in this folder.
   Do not also put your original part 3 or part 4 code in here.
   Every kernel module in this folder gets loaded, so both would run at the same time.

3. You do not need to write a Makefile. The one in the folder finds your files by itself.
   A file with module_init is built and loaded as a kernel module.
   A file with main() is built and run as a program.
   Use one kernel module, one program, or one of each.

4. Header files go in the include folder, not in partAdditional.
   Include them the same way part 3 does:
       #include "../include/address_map_arm_vm.h"

5. Test it with ./runall.sh additional
   This loads your kernel module first (if you have one).
   Then it loads the part 2 driver (/dev/KEY, /dev/SW) and your part 3 driver (/dev/LEDR, /dev/HEX).
   If your own module already makes one of those /dev files, the matching driver is skipped.
   Then it runs your program (if you have one).
   To see what your module prints with printk, run: dmesg | tail

6. Fill in the ADDITIONAL PART section at the bottom of this README.
   I'll use it to find and test your work, so be specific.


To clean all directories and remove all drivers: ./runall clean


To submit your solution: ./runall.sh submit 
The script generates a .tar.bz2 archive. Please upload this
archive to quercus (only one team member needs to do this).


The script may generate errors, for example:
ERROR: Removing 'timer': No such file or directory
These errors can be ignored.


If you would like to clarify your interpretation of the exercise instructions 
or provide any additional information to the grader, PLEASE TYPE IT HERE:



ADDITIONAL PART:
Which part it is based on (3 or 4): Part 4

What I added:
I extended Part 4 into a multi-function accumulator. Instead of having every
pushbutton perform the same operation, each KEY now performs a different
operation on the running total.

How to use it (which KEYs and switches do what, what shows on the LEDs, HEX or terminal):
Set SW9-SW0 to the desired input value.

KEY0: Adds the current SW value to the running total.
KEY1: Subtracts the current SW value from the running total. The total is
      limited to a minimum value of 0.
KEY2: Resets the running total to 0.
KEY3: Sets the running total directly to the current SW value.

Whenever any KEY is pressed, LEDR9-LEDR0 show the current SW value and
HEX5-HEX0 show the resulting running total as a six-digit decimal number.
The terminal also prints the operation, SW value, and resulting total.
